minute hand when you start; read the elapsed time against the bezel. Try it with a parking meter, a pot of pasta or a meeting.</p><label for="bz-s" class="rt-lab">Set bezel to start at minute <output id="bz-sv">10</output></label><input type="range" id="bz-s" min="0" max="59" value="10"><label for="bz-e" class="rt-lab">Minutes elapsed <output id="bz-ev">12</output></label><input type="range" id="bz-e" min="0" max="60" value="12"><p class="rt-bz-o" id="bz-o" aria-live="polite"></p><p class="rt-small">Dive bezels turn one way only, so a knock can only make the time look longer, never shorter.</p></div></div></section>

<section class="rt-s" id="week"><div class="rt-w"><div class="rt-sh"><p class="rt-k">The week planner</p><h2>Which watch, which day?</h2><p class="rt-lead">Tick the watches you own and what your week holds. We will spread them across the days so each one gets worn.</p></div><div class="rt-wk"><fieldset class="rt-fs"><legend>My watches</legend><label><input type="checkbox" value="dress" checked> Dress watch</label><label><input type="checkbox" value="diver" checked> Diver or sports</label><label><input type="checkbox" value="field" checked> Field or everyday</label><label><input type="checkbox" value="chrono"> Chronograph</label><label><input type="checkbox" value="digital"> Digital or smart</label></fieldset><fieldset class="rt-fs"><legend>This week</legend><div class="rt-days" id="wk-d"></div></fieldset></div><ol class="rt-cal" id="wk-o" aria-live="polite"></ol></div></section>

<section class="rt-s rt-cream2"><div class="rt-w rt-pr"><div><p class="rt-k">Power reserve</p><h2>When will it stop?</h2><p>An automatic or hand-wound watch keeps running for its power reserve after you take it off. Enter when you last wore it to see when it will stop, and whether to set it again before your next wear.</p><label for="pr-h" class="rt-lab">Power reserve<select id="pr-h"><option value="38">38 hours (many older calibres)</option><option value="42" selected>42 hours</option><option value="50">50 hours</option><option value="70">70 hours</option><option value="80">80 hours</option></select></label><label for="pr-t" class="rt-lab">Taken off<input type="datetime-local" id="pr-t"></label></div><div class="rt-tank"><div class="rt-tank-b"><i id="pr-f"></i></div><div id="pr-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div></div></section>

<section class="rt-s"><div class="rt-w rt-box"><div><p class="rt-k">The watch box</p><h2>Three slots, three moods</h2><p>A small rotation does not need to be expensive. Most people are covered by one smart watch, one tough watch and one they simply love.</p></div><div class="rt-slots"><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1612817159450-08a180df028b?auto=format&fit=crop&w=300&h=360&q=75" alt="a silver round analogue watch with a brown leather strap" width="300" height="360" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Smart</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1612817159576-986a0b7a4165?auto=format&fit=crop&w=300&h=360&q=75" alt="a silver and black analogue watch" width="300" height="360" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Tough</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1766518303334-aaa928ceed45?auto=format&fit=crop&w=300&h=360&q=75" alt="a close-up of a gold and silver watch" width="300" height="360" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Loved</figcaption></figure></div></div></section>

<section class="rt-s rt-ox"><div class="rt-w rt-wind"><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1777891699620-98471a1042e2?auto=format&fit=crop&w=420&h=520&q=75" alt="close-up of intricate clockwork gears and a clock face" width="420" height="520" loading="lazy"></figure><div><p class="rt-k">Winders, simply</p><h2>Do you need a winder?</h2><dl class="rt-qa"><dt>Probably not, if&hellip;</dt><dd>you have a simple time-only or date watch. Setting it takes seconds, and a rest does it no harm.</dd><dt>Maybe, if&hellip;</dt><dd>you own a perpetual calendar, moon phase or complicated date that is slow to set.</dd><dt>Settings</dt><dd>Check the maker&rsquo;s recommended turns per day and direction. Many automatics are happy at around 650&ndash;900 turns a day in both directions.</dd></dl></div><figure class="rt-wind-2"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1548907228-419a3f3d11d0?auto=format&fit=crop&w=420&h=300&q=75" alt="a round black chronograph watch in a case" width="420" height="300" loading="lazy"></figure></div></section>

<section class="rt-s"><div class="rt-w rt-feat"><a href="watch-rotation-guide.html"><span class="rt-num">01</span><small>Feature</small><b>Building a Watch Rotation</b><em>How many watches you need, how to choose them and how to wear them all.</em></a><a href="automatic-watch-winding-guide.html"><span class="rt-num">02</span><small>Feature</small><b>Automatic Watch Winding</b><em>Hand-winding, setting the date safely and when a winder makes sense.</em></a></div></section>

